            <ArticleTitle>Cervical epidural analgesia for a cancer child at home.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>58-60</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A cervical epidural catheter was placed in a 32-month-old girl suffering from painful bone metastases of a relapsing neuroblastoma in the left forearm. This therapeutic approach was chosen because oral morphine had failed to provide analgesia while producing intolerable side effects. The epidural injection of a mixture of morphine and bupivacaine provided complete analgesia with no side effects. This enabled the parents to take care of their child at home until the end, 35 days later.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
